===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|rapen}}, {{m|enm|rappen||to abduct; ravish; seduce; rape; seize; snatch; carry off; transport}}, probably from {{der|en|la|rapere}} (verb), possibly through or influenced by {{der|en|xno|-}} {{m|fro|rap}}, {{m|fro|rape}} (noun) (compare also {{m|en|ravish}}). But compare {{cog|sv|rappa||to snatch, seize, carry off}}, {{cog|nds|rapen||to snatch, seize}}, {{cog|nl|rapen||to pick up, gather, collect}}; the relationship with Germanic forms is not clear. Cognate with {{cog|lt|reikėti||to be in need}}. Compare also {{m|en|rap||seize, snatch}}.<ref>"rape, v.2" and "rape, n.3" in the OED Online (Oxford University Press), [http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/158153], [http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/158145] (accessed September 12, 2012)</ref> Further, some senses may be from {{l|en|rape|id=haste|3=Etymology 3}}, an Old Norse word.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of forcing [[sex]] upon another [[person]] without their [[consent]] or against their [[will]]; originally [[coitus]] forced by a man on a woman, but now generally any [[sex act]] forced by any person upon another person; by extension, any [[non-consensual]] sex act forced on or perpetrated by any [[being]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|text=I fled; but he pursued (though more, it seems, <br> Inflamed with lust than rage), and, swifter far, <br> Me overtook, his mother, all dismayed, <br> And, in embraces forcible and foul <br> Engendering with me, of that '''rape''' begot <br> These yelling monsters {{...}}|book=II|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=‘Turning Victims into Saints’|journal=Time|date=22 Jan 1990
|passage=Last April the media world exploded in indignation at the '''rape''' and beating of a jogger in Central Park.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=William Butler Yeats|title=The Collected Works of W.B. Yeats Volume XIII: A Vision: The Original 1925 Version|publisher=Simon and Schuster|isbn=9781416593737
|passage=Castor and Pollux are one set of twins birthed by Leda after her '''rape''' by Zeus in swan form; {{...}}}}
#* {{seeCites|en}}
# {{lb|en|slang|sometimes|offensive}} An experience that is pleasant for one party and unpleasant for the other, particularly when the unwilling partner's suffering is worse than necessary.
## Overpowerment; utter defeat.
## {{rfc-sense|en}} An insult to one's senses so severe that one feels that they cannot ever be the same afterwards.
##:{{usex|en|The ear '''rape''' of that concert was so bad I can't even listen to their songs at work anymore.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} The taking of something by [[force]]; [[seizure]], [[plunder]]. {{defdate|from early 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''Rape''' of [[Nanking]]}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1638|author=w:George Sandys|title=A Paraphrase upon Job|chapter=XXII
|passage=Ruin'd orphans of thy '''rapes''' complain.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1712|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=The '''Rape''' of the Lock}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1959|author=w:Dorothy Parker|chapter=Ellery Queen: ''The New York Murders''|title=The Portable Dorothy Parker|year_published=1976|location=New York|publisher=Penguin|pages=566–8
|passage=Ellery Queen deals entirely in murders; you are not fobbed off, as you are with Mr. Leslie Charteris's ''Saint'', with pablum about the '''rape''' of the dowager's emeralds, or the theft of the blueprint of the newest submarine.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Lawrence Durrell|title=[[w:Monsieur (novel)|Monsieur or The Prince of Darkness: A Novel]]|location=London|publisher=w:Faber and Faber|year=1974|isbn=978-0-571-10660-8|newversion=republished in|title2=[[w:The Avignon Quintet|The Avignon Quintet: Monsieur, Livia, Constance, Sebastian, Quinx]]|location2=London|publisher2=Faber and Faber|year2=1992|page2=23|isbn2=978-0-571-16328-1|passage=She worked under the great tapestry with its glowing but subdued tones—huntsmen with lofted horns had been running down a female stag. After the '''rape''', leaving the grooms to bring the trophy home, they galloped away into the soft brumous Italian skyline; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1977|author=w:JRR Tolkien|title=The Silmarillion
|passage=Few of the Teleri were willing to go forth to war, for they remembered the slaying at the Swanhaven, and the '''rape''' of their ships.}}
# {{lb|en|now|archaic}} The [[abduction]] of a woman, especially for sexual purposes. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus|I|1|text=''Sat.'' Traytor, if Rome haue law, or we haue power, <br> Thou and thy Faction shall repent this '''Rape'''. <br> ''Bass.'' '''Rape''' call you it my Lord, to cease my owne, <br> My true betrothed Loue, and now my wife?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Mary Beard|journal=The Guardian|date=8 Sep 2000
|passage=The tale of the '''rape''' of Lucretia, for example, is hardly tellable - as many Roman writers themselves discovered - without raising the question of where seduction ends and rape begins; the '''rape''' of the Sabines puts a similar question mark over the distinction between rape and marriage.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} That which is snatched away.
#* {{RQ:Sandys Psalmes|passage=Where now are all my hopes? O, never more. / Shall they revive! nor death her '''rapes''' restore.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Movement, as in snatching; haste; hurry.

=====Usage notes=====
In legal contexts, the definition of the crime of rape can have a significantly narrower scope than in common modern parlance.<ref>{{cite-web|en|date=7/19/2023|last=Kaplan|first=Lewis A.|authorlink=Lewis A. Kaplan|title=MEMORANDUM OPINION DENYING DEFENDANT’S RULE 59 MOTION|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230720131821/https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.nysd.590045/gov.uscourts.nysd.590045.212.0_1.pdf|work={{w|United States District Court for the Southern District of New York}}|page=3}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|en|year=2013|last=Freedman|first=Estelle B.|authorlink=Estelle Freedman|chapter=Introduction|title=Redefining Rape: Sexual Violence in the Era of Suffrage and Segregation|url=https://archive.org/details/redefiningrapese0000free/|publisher={{w|Harvard University Press}}|ISBN=978-0-674-72484-6|lccn=2013002883|page=4|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/redefiningrapese0000free/page/4/}}</ref>

