===Etymology 2===
Generally considered to derive from {{der|en|ang|rāp||rope}}, in reference to the ropes used to delineate the courts that ruled each rape.<ref name="EPNS-AMetc">{{cite-book|last=Mawer|first=Allen, F. M. Stenton with J. E. B. Gover|title=Sussex - Part I and Part II|publisher=English Place-Name Society|year=1929, 1930}}</ref><!-- Ropes were not used to delineate the entire territory of each rape. See John Horace Round ''The rapes and their origin'' in ''Archaeological Review'', volume 1 (1888), page 229--> Compare {{cog|nl|reep}} and the parish of {{w|Rope, Cheshire}}.

In the 18th century, {{w|Edward Lye}} proposed derivation from {{der|en|non|hreppr||tract of land}}, but this was rejected by the ''New English Dictionary'' and is considered "phonologically impossible" by the {{w|English Place-Name Society}}.<ref name="EPNS-AMetc"/> Others, considering it improbable that the Normans would have adopted a local word, suggest derivation from {{der|en|fro|raper||take by force}}.<ref name=castles>{{cite-web|url=http://www.sussexcastles.com/sussex-timeline/1067-origin-of-the-sussex.html|title=Origin of the Sussex 'Rapes'|publisher=Sussex Castles|accessdate=2015|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190419070511/http://sussexcastles.com/sussex-timeline/1067-origin-of-the-sussex.html|archivedate=19 April 2019}}</ref>

See {{w|Rape (county subdivision)|Wikipedia}} for more.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|now|historical}} One of the six former [[administrative]] [[divisions]] of [[Sussex]], England. {{defdate|from 11th c.}}
#* '''1888''' March 20, Henry H. Howorth, in a letter to ''The Archaeological Review'', volume 1 (March–August 1888), page 230:
#*: It seems to me very clear that the '''rapes''' of Sussex were divisions already existing there when the Normans landed.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author=Frank Merry Stenton|title=Anglo-Saxon England|passage=There is little, if any, doubt that the division of Sussex into six '''rapes''' had been carried out before the Conquest, though the term is not mentioned in any Old English record.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Ann Williams|title=The English and the Norman Conquest|page=18|passage=These four castles dominated the Sussex '''rapes''' named after them; the fifth '''rape''', Bramber, held by William de Braose, was in existence by 1084.}}

